Using $f(x)=x-1+g(x)$ in both conditions yields
\begin{align*}
g \left( \frac{1}{x + y} \right) &= g \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) + g \left( \frac{1}{y} \right), \\
(x + y) \left( x - 1 + g(x + y) \right) &= xy(x - 1 + g(x))(y - 1 + g(y)).
\end{align*}
Thus the first relation has exactly the same functional-equation form for $g$ as for $f$.

The substitution produces the pair
\begin{align*}
g \left( \frac{1}{x + y} \right) &= g \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) + g \left( \frac{1}{y} \right), \\
(x + y) \left( x - 1 + g(x + y) \right) &= xy(x - 1 + g(x))(y - 1 + g(y)).
\end{align*}
In particular, the first displayed identity says that $g$ satisfies the same functional equation as $f$.

